2141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 407 0 R
/T (Short answer: Jenkins now uses the terms controller and agent instead of master and slave. The controller is the brain: it serves the UI, stores configuration, manages plugins and credentials, and schedules builds. Agents are the workers: they run the actual build steps. In production, builds should run only on agents.)
/E (Short answer: Jenkins now uses the terms controller and agent instead of master and slave. The controller is the brain: it serves the UI, stores configuration, manages plugins and credentials, and schedules builds. Agents are the workers: they run the actual build steps. In production, builds should run only on agents.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
2142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 407 0 R
/T (Comparison)
/E (Comparison)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
2143 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 407 0 R
/T (How they talk: the controller sends build instructions to the agent through the remoting channel \(over SSH, TCP or WebSocket\), and the agent sends logs and results back. Hands-on experience: In my project, the controller ran on one EC2 instance with an EBS volume for JENKINS_HOME. All builds ran on agents. When an agent had issues, we just took it offline and replaced it, without any impact on Jenkins itself.)
/E (How they talk: the controller sends build instructions to the agent through the remoting channel \(over SSH, TCP or WebSocket\), and the agent sends logs and results back. Hands-on experience: In my project, the controller ran on one EC2 instance with an EBS volume for JENKINS_HOME. All builds ran on agents. When an agent had issues, we just took it offline and replaced it, without any impact on Jenkins itself.)
/Pg 65 0 R
/K [14]
>>
endobj
2144 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 407 0 R
/T (51. What is the difference between master and slave \(controller and agent\) in Jenkins?)
